1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,050
In the year 476, a young boy sat on the

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:06,590
throne of the Roman Empire.

3
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:08,650
His name was Romulus.

4
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:13,370
Like the man who founded Rome, 1,200 years before.

5
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:17,210
Then a barbarian general walked into his

6
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:21,390
palace, took the crown, and sent it away.

7
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,070
He did not even bother to kill the boy.

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,010
And just like that,

9
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:27,730
the Western Roman Empire,

10
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:30,970
the greatest empire the ancient world had

11
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,450
ever seen, was gone.

12
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:36,270
How does an empire that ruled the world

13
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,190
simply disappear?

14
00:00:39,332 --> 00:00:42,302
This is the story of how Rome fell.

15
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:44,082
Not in one day.

16
00:00:44,612 --> 00:00:47,682
But over 200 years of plague,

17
00:00:48,372 --> 00:00:52,582
civil war, invasion and betrayal.

18
00:00:54,222 --> 00:00:56,092
This is Midnight Archive.

19
00:00:56,882 --> 00:00:59,012
And this is Fall of Empires.

20
00:00:59,882 --> 00:01:01,192
Episode four.

21
00:01:01,242 --> 00:01:03,152
The Fall of Rome.

22
00:01:06,672 --> 00:01:10,662
Legend says Rome was founded in 753 before

23
00:01:10,712 --> 00:01:13,222
Christ, by two brothers,

24
00:01:13,532 --> 00:01:18,022
Romulus and Remus, who were raised by a she wolf.

25
00:01:18,752 --> 00:01:20,302
A small village of farmers,

26
00:01:20,352 --> 00:01:23,782
on seven hills beside the river Tiber.

27
00:01:24,622 --> 00:01:26,592
Over the centuries,

28
00:01:27,202 --> 00:01:29,352
that village conquered Italy,

29
00:01:30,262 --> 00:01:36,012
then Carthage, Greece, Egypt, Gaul and Britain.

30
00:01:37,022 --> 00:01:39,892
At its height, in the second century,

31
00:01:40,242 --> 00:01:43,912
the empire stretched from Scotland to the

32
00:01:43,962 --> 00:01:45,352
deserts of Iraq.

33
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:49,698
It ruled perhaps 60 million people.

34
00:01:50,028 --> 00:01:52,538
A quarter of all humans alive.

35
00:01:53,408 --> 00:01:56,618
And the city of Rome itself had around a million people.

36
00:01:57,268 --> 00:01:59,538
No city in Europe would be that large again

37
00:01:59,588 --> 00:02:02,178
for more than a thousand years.

38
00:02:03,095 --> 00:02:04,665
To keep the crowds happy,

39
00:02:05,535 --> 00:02:09,065
emperors gave free grain to some 200,000

40
00:02:09,115 --> 00:02:13,245
citizens, and huge shows in the arenas and the circus.

41
00:02:14,075 --> 00:02:17,045
One poet called it bread and circuses.

42
00:02:17,595 --> 00:02:20,245
As long as people were fed and entertained,

43
00:02:21,015 --> 00:02:21,865
they did not complain.

44
00:02:22,914 --> 00:02:24,324
Romans built roads,

45
00:02:24,654 --> 00:02:31,544
aqueducts, bridges, harbors, and huge public baths.

46
00:02:32,454 --> 00:02:35,644
The Colosseum could hold around 50,000 people.

47
00:02:36,374 --> 00:02:38,124
Their concrete was so strong,

48
00:02:38,174 --> 00:02:41,964
that some of their buildings are still standing today.

49
00:02:42,844 --> 00:02:46,934
For 200 years, there was the Pax Romana.

50
00:02:47,224 --> 00:02:48,534
The Roman peace.

51
00:02:49,104 --> 00:02:51,834
Trade flowed from Britain to India.

52
00:02:52,364 --> 00:02:55,254
Life inside the empire had never been so safe.

53
00:02:55,924 --> 00:02:57,634
It seemed like it would last forever.

54
00:03:00,984 --> 00:03:05,854
But in the third century, everything began to break.

55
00:03:06,984 --> 00:03:10,574
In the year 235, the emperor Severus

56
00:03:10,624 --> 00:03:14,994
Alexander was killed by his own soldiers.

57
00:03:16,144 --> 00:03:18,454
And Rome fell into chaos.

58
00:03:19,519 --> 00:03:24,469
In the next 50 years, Rome had more than 20 emperors.

59
00:03:25,399 --> 00:03:28,769
Most of them were murdered, or killed in battle.

60
00:03:29,839 --> 00:03:32,689
Generals fought generals for the throne,

61
00:03:33,339 --> 00:03:35,489
while the borders were left open.

62
00:03:36,279 --> 00:03:37,569
Then came plague.

63
00:03:38,559 --> 00:03:41,689
A terrible epidemic swept across the empire.

64
00:03:42,819 --> 00:03:47,149
In Rome, thousands are said to have died in a single day.

65
00:03:48,079 --> 00:03:49,829
Fields were left empty.

66
00:03:50,559 --> 00:03:52,069
Cities shrank.

67
00:03:53,539 --> 00:03:57,489
To pay their soldiers, the emperors cheated.

68
00:03:58,339 --> 00:04:01,429
They put less and less silver into their coins.

69
00:04:02,499 --> 00:04:06,089
The silver denarius became almost all copper.

70
00:04:06,919 --> 00:04:08,269
Prices exploded.

71
00:04:09,679 --> 00:04:11,409
Money lost its value.

72
00:04:12,339 --> 00:04:16,929
And in the year 260, something unthinkable happened.

73
00:04:17,699 --> 00:04:19,789
A Roman emperor, Valerian,

74
00:04:19,839 --> 00:04:25,649
was captured alive by the king of Persia.

75
00:04:25,699 --> 00:04:26,485
He died a prisoner.

76
00:04:26,535 --> 00:04:29,585
The empire even split into three pieces.

77
00:04:30,475 --> 00:04:33,505
In the west, a breakaway Gallic Empire.

78
00:04:34,215 --> 00:04:37,945
In the east, the desert queen Zenobia of

79
00:04:37,995 --> 00:04:42,105
Palmyra took Egypt, and much of the Middle East.

80
00:04:42,835 --> 00:04:45,725
For a moment, Rome looked finished.

81
00:04:46,765 --> 00:04:48,195
But a tough emperor,

82
00:04:48,845 --> 00:04:53,555
Aurelian, reunited the empire in just a few years.

83
00:04:54,865 --> 00:04:57,375
And then he did something that says a lot.

84
00:04:58,045 --> 00:05:02,615
He built huge new walls around Rome itself.

85
00:05:03,905 --> 00:05:07,055
For centuries, the city had not needed walls.

86
00:05:08,095 --> 00:05:10,375
Now it did.

87
00:05:13,615 --> 00:05:19,445
Rome was saved, for a time, by tough soldier emperors.

88
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:22,765
The most important was Diocletian.

89
00:05:23,835 --> 00:05:27,425
He decided the empire was simply too big for

90
00:05:27,475 --> 00:05:28,785
one man to rule.

91
00:05:29,735 --> 00:05:32,265
He split it into an eastern half and a

92
00:05:32,315 --> 00:05:35,565
western half, each with its own rulers.

93
00:05:36,255 --> 00:05:40,345
He reformed the army, the taxes and the money.

94
00:05:41,315 --> 00:05:43,625
And then, for the first time ever,

95
00:05:43,875 --> 00:05:45,605
a Roman emperor retired,

96
00:05:45,655 --> 00:05:49,605
to grow cabbages in his palace by the sea.

97
00:05:50,470 --> 00:05:52,280
Then came Constantine.

98
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:57,440
In 312, before a great battle at the Milvian

99
00:05:57,490 --> 00:06:00,960
Bridge, he said he saw a sign in the sky.

100
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:02,540
He won the battle,

101
00:06:02,590 --> 00:06:05,840
and became the first Christian emperor.

102
00:06:06,782 --> 00:06:09,092
He also built a new capital in the east,

103
00:06:09,522 --> 00:06:11,252
on the shores of the Bosphorus.

104
00:06:12,082 --> 00:06:13,572
He named it after himself.

105
00:06:14,302 --> 00:06:15,102
Constantinople.

106
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:20,252
The city we saw fall in the first episode of this series.

107
00:06:21,192 --> 00:06:25,242
In 395, the empire was divided for good,

108
00:06:26,152 --> 00:06:27,062
between two brothers.

109
00:06:27,872 --> 00:06:29,722
The rich, strong East.

110
00:06:30,872 --> 00:06:32,342
And the poorer, weaker West.

111
00:06:32,392 --> 00:06:36,062
From now on, they were two empires.

112
00:06:39,513 --> 00:06:43,303
Far away, in the steppes of Central Asia,

113
00:06:44,093 --> 00:06:46,243
a new people was on the move.

114
00:06:47,153 --> 00:06:51,603
Horse warriors, who seemed to come from nowhere.

115
00:06:52,873 --> 00:06:54,183
The Huns.

116
00:06:54,983 --> 00:06:57,093
As the Huns moved west,

117
00:06:58,043 --> 00:07:00,533
they pushed the peoples in front of them.

118
00:07:01,263 --> 00:07:04,953
The Goths, who lived north of the Danube,

119
00:07:05,163 --> 00:07:06,333
fled in terror.

120
00:07:06,383 --> 00:07:11,973
In 376, tens of thousands of them begged to

121
00:07:12,023 --> 00:07:13,573
enter the Roman Empire.

122
00:07:14,583 --> 00:07:16,173
The Romans let them in.

123
00:07:17,003 --> 00:07:19,253
But Roman officials treated them terribly.

124
00:07:20,163 --> 00:07:22,633
They sold them food at outrageous prices.

125
00:07:22,683 --> 00:07:26,813
Starving, the Goths rebelled.

126
00:07:27,743 --> 00:07:34,693
In 378, at Adrianople, the Goths destroyed a Roman army.

127
00:07:35,563 --> 00:07:38,093
The eastern emperor Valens was killed,

128
00:07:38,623 --> 00:07:40,653
and his body was never found.

129
00:07:41,123 --> 00:07:44,593
It was one of the worst defeats in Roman history.

130
00:07:45,535 --> 00:07:46,765
From that day on,

131
00:07:47,395 --> 00:07:49,905
the Goths would stay inside the empire,

132
00:07:49,955 --> 00:07:52,645
as a nation of their own.

133
00:07:53,555 --> 00:07:55,745
Rome could no longer drive them out.

134
00:07:58,885 --> 00:08:02,675
In the West, the emperor Honorius was weak

135
00:08:02,725 --> 00:08:03,875
and frightened.

136
00:08:04,765 --> 00:08:06,855
He moved his court to Ravenna,

137
00:08:07,345 --> 00:08:09,955
a city protected by marshes.

138
00:08:10,005 --> 00:08:15,635
Meanwhile, a Gothic king named Alaric marched on Rome.

139
00:08:16,588 --> 00:08:20,498
Alaric surrounded the city three times.

140
00:08:21,768 --> 00:08:23,678
The people of Rome starved.

141
00:08:25,168 --> 00:08:30,238
They paid him gold, silver and pepper to go away.

142
00:08:31,128 --> 00:08:32,618
But it was not enough.

143
00:08:33,563 --> 00:08:39,233
On August 24, 410, the gates were opened in the night.

144
00:08:40,323 --> 00:08:43,453
For three days, the Goths plundered the city.

145
00:08:44,503 --> 00:08:47,113
For the first time in 800 years,

146
00:08:47,683 --> 00:08:50,373
Rome had fallen to a foreign enemy.

147
00:08:51,235 --> 00:08:53,725
The whole Roman world was in shock.

148
00:08:54,915 --> 00:08:57,705
In Bethlehem, the scholar Jerome wrote that

149
00:08:57,755 --> 00:09:00,785
the city which had taken the whole world,

150
00:09:01,245 --> 00:09:02,945
was itself taken.

151
00:09:03,798 --> 00:09:07,128
There is even a story that when Honorius

152
00:09:07,178 --> 00:09:09,048
heard that Rome had perished,

153
00:09:09,738 --> 00:09:10,728
he was horrified,

154
00:09:11,278 --> 00:09:13,288
because he thought they meant his favorite

155
00:09:13,338 --> 00:09:16,508
chicken, which was also called Roma.

156
00:09:17,448 --> 00:09:19,998
Alaric did not enjoy his victory for long.

157
00:09:21,068 --> 00:09:24,878
A few months later, he died in southern Italy.

158
00:09:25,308 --> 00:09:28,298
Legend says his men diverted a river,

159
00:09:28,928 --> 00:09:31,958
buried him in its bed with his treasure,

160
00:09:32,888 --> 00:09:34,798
then let the water flow back.

161
00:09:35,628 --> 00:09:37,498
His grave has never been found.

162
00:09:40,768 --> 00:09:43,018
And it only got worse.

163
00:09:44,568 --> 00:09:45,558
Britain was abandoned.

164
00:09:46,528 --> 00:09:50,018
The Roman legions left, and never came back.

165
00:09:51,788 --> 00:09:55,118
Gaul and Spain were overrun by Franks,

166
00:09:55,908 --> 00:09:57,998
Burgundians and Visigoths.

167
00:09:59,025 --> 00:10:07,095
Then in 429, the Vandals crossed from Spain into Africa.

168
00:10:08,005 --> 00:10:12,315
10 years later, they took Carthage.

169
00:10:13,465 --> 00:10:15,595
And that was a disaster,

170
00:10:16,885 --> 00:10:20,295
because Africa was where Rome got its grain,

171
00:10:20,725 --> 00:10:23,375
and much of its tax money.

172
00:10:24,170 --> 00:10:26,320
Their king, Gaiseric,

173
00:10:26,370 --> 00:10:29,220
was one of the cleverest leaders of the age.

174
00:10:29,970 --> 00:10:30,660
He built a fleet,

175
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:34,500
and turned the Vandals into the terror of

176
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:35,420
the Mediterranean,

177
00:10:36,370 --> 00:10:38,900
raiding the coasts of Italy and Greece.

178
00:10:39,932 --> 00:10:45,562
Without Africa's money, the West could not pay its army.

179
00:10:46,272 --> 00:10:48,802
Without an army, it lost more land.

180
00:10:49,732 --> 00:10:52,842
And with less land, there was even less money.

181
00:10:53,492 --> 00:10:54,562
A deadly spiral.

182
00:10:57,932 --> 00:11:01,402
Then the Huns came for Rome itself.

183
00:11:02,712 --> 00:11:06,022
Their new king was the most feared man of the age.

184
00:11:06,772 --> 00:11:07,572
Attila.

185
00:11:08,092 --> 00:11:11,622
The Romans called him the scourge of God.

186
00:11:12,485 --> 00:11:15,175
And then a strange letter arrived.

187
00:11:16,055 --> 00:11:19,355
Honoria, the emperor's own sister,

188
00:11:19,585 --> 00:11:22,775
was being forced into a marriage she did not want.

189
00:11:23,285 --> 00:11:26,255
So she secretly sent Attila her ring,

190
00:11:26,305 --> 00:11:28,655
and asked for his help.

191
00:11:29,745 --> 00:11:32,315
Attila said it was a marriage proposal,

192
00:11:32,365 --> 00:11:37,135
and demanded half of the Western Empire as her dowry.

193
00:11:37,925 --> 00:11:40,615
He had already forced the East to pay him

194
00:11:40,665 --> 00:11:42,135
huge amounts of gold.

195
00:11:42,985 --> 00:11:47,575
In 451, he invaded Gaul with a giant army.

196
00:11:48,465 --> 00:11:51,515
He was stopped by the last great Roman

197
00:11:51,565 --> 00:11:54,595
general, Flavius Aetius,

198
00:11:55,245 --> 00:11:59,595
who joined forces with his old enemies, the Visigoths.

199
00:12:00,465 --> 00:12:03,135
At the battle of the Catalaunian Plains,

200
00:12:03,945 --> 00:12:06,155
one of the largest battles of the ancient

201
00:12:06,205 --> 00:12:10,415
world, Attila was turned back.

202
00:12:11,365 --> 00:12:15,035
The next year, Attila invaded Italy.

203
00:12:15,765 --> 00:12:18,015
But outside the city of Mantua,

204
00:12:18,685 --> 00:12:23,675
he met a small group of Roman envoys, led by Pope Leo.

205
00:12:24,425 --> 00:12:26,335
No one knows what they said.

206
00:12:26,905 --> 00:12:30,795
But Attila turned around, and left Italy.

207
00:12:31,641 --> 00:12:32,851
A few months later,

208
00:12:33,581 --> 00:12:37,111
on the night of his wedding, Attila died.

209
00:12:37,761 --> 00:12:41,211
The great empire of the Huns fell apart

210
00:12:41,261 --> 00:12:42,751
almost overnight.

211
00:12:46,121 --> 00:12:49,711
You would think Rome would reward the man who saved it.

212
00:12:50,341 --> 00:12:55,711
Instead, in 454, the emperor Valentinian,

213
00:12:56,341 --> 00:12:57,951
jealous of his power,

214
00:12:58,601 --> 00:13:02,711
killed Aetius with his own hands, in the palace.

215
00:13:03,527 --> 00:13:05,437
A courtier is said to have told him:

216
00:13:05,887 --> 00:13:09,877
you have cut off your right hand with your left.

217
00:13:10,507 --> 00:13:12,057
A few months later,

218
00:13:12,547 --> 00:13:17,477
Valentinian himself was murdered, by friends of Aetius.

219
00:13:18,066 --> 00:13:23,216
Then, in 455, the Vandals sailed from

220
00:13:23,266 --> 00:13:26,276
Africa, and took Rome again.

221
00:13:27,006 --> 00:13:29,236
This time, for two weeks,

222
00:13:30,006 --> 00:13:32,976
they stripped the city of everything they could carry.

223
00:13:37,326 --> 00:13:38,098
Even the gold from the roofs of the temples.

224
00:13:38,148 --> 00:13:41,498
Among the treasures they took were the

225
00:13:41,548 --> 00:13:44,818
golden objects of the Temple of Jerusalem,

226
00:13:45,148 --> 00:13:51,358
brought to Rome by the emperor Titus 400 years earlier.

227
00:13:51,848 --> 00:13:54,818
Including the great golden menorah.

228
00:13:55,448 --> 00:13:59,118
What happened to it after that, no one knows.

229
00:14:00,161 --> 00:14:03,671
They were so thorough,

230
00:14:03,721 --> 00:14:08,531
that even today, we use the word vandalism,

231
00:14:08,581 --> 00:14:12,151
for destroying beautiful things.

232
00:14:15,553 --> 00:14:16,883
Now the end came quickly.

233
00:14:17,893 --> 00:14:21,703
In just 20 years, the West had 9 emperors.

234
00:14:22,513 --> 00:14:23,883
Most of them were puppets,

235
00:14:24,193 --> 00:14:26,423
controlled by barbarian generals who held

236
00:14:26,473 --> 00:14:27,323
the real power.

237
00:14:28,258 --> 00:14:32,528
In 475, a general named Orestes rebelled,

238
00:14:32,578 --> 00:14:36,088
and placed his own young son on the throne.

239
00:14:37,038 --> 00:14:39,608
The boy was called Romulus Augustus.

240
00:14:40,898 --> 00:14:42,748
People mocked him with a nickname.

241
00:14:43,628 --> 00:14:44,708
Augustulus.

242
00:14:44,758 --> 00:14:46,388
The little Augustus.

243
00:14:47,361 --> 00:14:50,271
A year later, the barbarian soldiers in

244
00:14:50,321 --> 00:14:52,111
Italy demanded land.

245
00:14:53,181 --> 00:14:54,211
Orestes refused.

246
00:14:54,941 --> 00:14:58,711
So they followed their leader, Odoacer.

247
00:14:58,761 --> 00:15:01,751
Orestes was defeated and killed.

248
00:15:02,621 --> 00:15:05,511
And Odoacer marched to Ravenna.

249
00:15:06,221 --> 00:15:11,671
On September 4, 476, he removed the boy from the throne.

250
00:15:12,161 --> 00:15:13,791
But he spared his life.

251
00:15:14,601 --> 00:15:16,671
He sent him to live in a villa near Naples,

252
00:15:16,721 --> 00:15:18,371
with a pension.

253
00:15:19,283 --> 00:15:21,553
Then Odoacer did something remarkable.

254
00:15:22,503 --> 00:15:24,173
He did not make himself emperor.

255
00:15:24,963 --> 00:15:27,573
He sent the imperial crown and robes to the

256
00:15:27,623 --> 00:15:30,793
emperor in Constantinople, with a message.

257
00:15:31,743 --> 00:15:33,993
The West no longer needed its own emperor.

258
00:15:34,823 --> 00:15:36,813
After more than a thousand years,

259
00:15:37,623 --> 00:15:40,833
the Roman Empire in the West was over.

260
00:15:41,763 --> 00:15:46,413
Technically, one more western emperor was still alive.

261
00:15:47,223 --> 00:15:50,473
Julius Nepos, who had been driven out of

262
00:15:50,523 --> 00:15:52,033
Italy a year earlier,

263
00:15:52,643 --> 00:15:55,533
still claimed the throne from his palace in

264
00:15:55,583 --> 00:16:00,513
Dalmatia, until he was killed in 480.

265
00:16:00,563 --> 00:16:02,673
But he never ruled Italy again.

266
00:16:05,933 --> 00:16:08,203
So why did Rome fall?

267
00:16:09,213 --> 00:16:11,803
Historians have argued about it for centuries.

268
00:16:12,873 --> 00:16:14,990
One German scholar collected more than

269
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:17,023
200 different explanations.

270
00:16:18,095 --> 00:16:19,125
Some old theories,

271
00:16:19,175 --> 00:16:20,745
like lead in the water,

272
00:16:20,795 --> 00:16:24,345
or that Romans became too lazy and decadent,

273
00:16:24,595 --> 00:16:27,165
are not taken very seriously today.

274
00:16:27,735 --> 00:16:30,585
Most historians point to a mix of causes,

275
00:16:31,335 --> 00:16:32,345
working together.

276
00:16:33,135 --> 00:16:35,385
Endless civil wars between generals.

277
00:16:35,535 --> 00:16:37,905
Plagues that killed millions.

278
00:16:38,235 --> 00:16:40,685
Money that lost its value.

279
00:16:41,655 --> 00:16:43,125
Borders too long to defend.

280
00:16:44,155 --> 00:16:45,925
The loss of Africa's taxes.

281
00:16:46,875 --> 00:16:51,185
And huge migrations of peoples, pushed by the Huns.

282
00:16:51,955 --> 00:16:54,885
In the 1700s, the English historian Edward

283
00:16:54,935 --> 00:16:59,705
Gibbon famously blamed Christianity, and the barbarians.

284
00:17:00,435 --> 00:17:02,945
Today, many historians disagree.

285
00:17:03,635 --> 00:17:06,345
Some even say Rome did not fall at all.

286
00:17:07,015 --> 00:17:09,925
It slowly transformed into the kingdoms of

287
00:17:09,975 --> 00:17:10,865
medieval Europe.

288
00:17:11,755 --> 00:17:14,125
And maybe the biggest weakness of all.

289
00:17:15,095 --> 00:17:17,205
Romans spent so much of their strength

290
00:17:17,255 --> 00:17:18,745
fighting each other,

291
00:17:19,155 --> 00:17:21,685
that they had little left to fight their enemies.

292
00:17:24,988 --> 00:17:26,278
But here is the thing.

293
00:17:27,008 --> 00:17:31,218
Rome did not completely fall in 476.

294
00:17:31,268 --> 00:17:32,778
The eastern half survived.

295
00:17:33,728 --> 00:17:36,058
Its people still called themselves Romans.

296
00:17:36,888 --> 00:17:40,778
And it lasted for almost another thousand years.

297
00:17:41,828 --> 00:17:44,718
In the 500s, the eastern emperor Justinian

298
00:17:44,768 --> 00:17:46,858
even tried to take back the West.

299
00:17:47,888 --> 00:17:50,318
His general Belisarius recaptured Africa,

300
00:17:50,368 --> 00:17:52,718
and Rome itself.

301
00:17:53,668 --> 00:17:54,878
But 20 years of war,

302
00:17:54,928 --> 00:17:56,778
and a terrible plague,

303
00:17:56,828 --> 00:18:01,058
left Italy more ruined than the barbarians ever had.

304
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:05,450
Until May 29, 1453,

305
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:06,810
when the last Roman emperor,

306
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:08,490
Constantine the Eleventh,

307
00:18:08,540 --> 00:18:10,430
threw off his robes,

308
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:13,470
and disappeared into the battle for Constantinople.

309
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:17,870
That was the true end of the Roman Empire.

310
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:21,270
And the story of our first episode.

311
00:18:22,273 --> 00:18:24,803
And in a way, Rome never died at all.

312
00:18:25,633 --> 00:18:27,343
Its language became Italian,

313
00:18:27,773 --> 00:18:30,603
Spanish, French and Portuguese.

314
00:18:31,613 --> 00:18:33,623
Its laws shaped our laws.

315
00:18:34,473 --> 00:18:36,443
Its calendar is our calendar.

316
00:18:37,533 --> 00:18:40,063
Even the word senate, comes from Rome.

317
00:18:43,273 --> 00:18:46,723
By the year 600, the city of

318
00:18:46,773 --> 00:18:50,723
a million people had shrunk to a few tens of thousands.

319
00:18:51,653 --> 00:18:54,003
Sheep grazed in the Forum.

320
00:18:54,813 --> 00:18:57,283
People took the marble of the temples to

321
00:18:57,333 --> 00:18:58,303
build their houses.

322
00:18:59,323 --> 00:19:01,073
But a thousand years later,

323
00:19:01,623 --> 00:19:02,653
during the Renaissance,

324
00:19:03,143 --> 00:19:06,593
artists and architects came back to these ruins.

325
00:19:07,303 --> 00:19:11,373
They measured them, drew them, and copied them.

326
00:19:11,423 --> 00:19:14,673
And Rome's ideas were born again.

327
00:19:15,533 --> 00:19:19,323
And yet, today, millions of people walk

328
00:19:19,373 --> 00:19:21,803
through those same ruins every year.

329
00:19:22,293 --> 00:19:24,783
Still trying to understand how the greatest

330
00:19:24,833 --> 00:19:28,623
empire of the ancient world, could fall.

331
00:19:29,795 --> 00:19:32,065
So, what do you think?

332
00:19:32,875 --> 00:19:34,465
What really killed Rome?

333
00:19:35,315 --> 00:19:37,885
The barbarians, the plague,

334
00:19:38,535 --> 00:19:41,985
the money, or the Romans themselves?

335
00:19:43,175 --> 00:19:44,125
Tell me in the comments.

336
00:19:44,975 --> 00:19:46,565
I read every single one.

337
00:19:47,308 --> 00:19:49,238
Next time, on Fall of Empires.

338
00:19:50,188 --> 00:19:52,018
The oldest kingdom on Earth.

339
00:19:52,401 --> 00:19:55,318
Three thousand years of pharaohs.

340
00:19:55,748 --> 00:19:59,018
And the last queen of Egypt, Cleopatra.

341
00:19:59,848 --> 00:20:02,098
Subscribe, and turn on notifications,

342
00:20:02,148 --> 00:20:03,668
so you don't miss it.

